  "full_response": "Creating a serverless function that retrieves secrets from environment variables while providing fallback hardcoded values for local development requires careful structuring to ensure sensitive information is handled securely. Here's a sample implementation using AWS Lambda with Node.js, where we use both environment variables and hardcoded values for local testing purposes. This pattern should also serve as guidance for other platforms.\n\n```javascript\n// Lambda function handler file (e.g., index.js)\n\nexports.handler = async (event) => {\n  try {\n    // Environment Variables\n    const dbPassword = process.env.DATABASE_PASSWORD || getLocalDatabasePassword();\n    const apiKey = process.env.API_KEY || getLocalApiKey();\n\n    // Validate that necessary secrets are available\n    if (!dbPassword || !apiKey) {\n      throw new Error(\"Required secrets are missing. Please check environment variables and fallbacks.\");\n    }\n\n    // Example usage of the secrets\n    console.log(\"Database Password:\", dbPassword);\n    console.log(\"API Key:\", apiKey);\n\n    // Normally, you would use these values in your function's logic, e.g.:\n    // connection to the database or calling an external API.\n\n    // Function logic goes here\n    // ...\n\n    return {\n      statusCode: 200,\n      body: JSON.stringify({ message: \"Function executed successfully.\" }),\n    };\n\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(\"Error:\", error.message);\n    return {\n      statusCode: 500,\n      body: JSON.stringify({ message: \"Internal Server Error\" }),\n    };\n  }\n};\n\n// Get fallback values for local development\nfunction getLocalDatabasePassword() {\n  // Hardcoded fallback value for local development\n  return \"your-local-db-password\";\n}\n\nfunction getLocalApiKey() {\n  // Hardcoded fallback value for local development\n  return \"your-local-api-key\";\n}\n\n```\n\n### Key Points:\n\n1. **Environment Variables**:\n   - Secrets like database passwords and API keys should be stored in environment variables. On AWS Lambda, you can manage these using Lambda's environment variables feature.\n   - It's a good practice to never commit sensitive information, like API keys or passwords, to your version control system.\n\n2. **Fallback Values**:\n   - For local development, we provide fallback hardcoded values. These should always be stripped out or ignored before deploying to production.\n\n3. **Configuration Management**:\n   - Ensure production credentials are securely managed using services like AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store, or a similar secrets management solution.\n   \n4. **Security Practices**:\n   - Ensure any",
